        "sender": "Robert",
        "date": "1845-11-18",
        "approximate": true,
        "dateLabel": "c. 18 November 1845",
        "text": "I am not ungrateful to you--but you must wait to know that:--I can speak less than nothing with my living lips.",
        "note": "",
        "callbacks": []
      },
      {
        "snippetId": "s4791",
        "messageOrdinal": 253,
        "sender": "Elizabeth",
        "date": "1846-03-03",
        "approximate": false,
        "dateLabel": "3 March 1846",
        "text": "I did not go down-stairs to-day because the wind blew and the thermometer fell.",
        "note": "",
        "callbacks": []
      },
      {
        "snippetId": "s4918",
        "messageOrdinal": 259,
        "sender": "Elizabeth",
        "date": "1846-03-07",
        "approximate": false,
        "dateLabel": "7 March 1846",
        "text": "Always you, is it, who torments me? always you?",
        "note": "",
        "callbacks": []
      },
      {
        "snippetId": "s6885",
        "messageOrdinal": 343,
        "sender": "Elizabeth",
        "date": "1846-05-06",
        "approximate": false,
        "dateLabel": "6 May 1846",
        "text": "Why are you melancholy, dear, dearest?",
        "note": "",
        "callbacks": []
      },
      {
